<TopicPage slug="subjects/political-science/intermediate/the-public-policy-cycle">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    The policy cycle is <strong>government's product development loop</strong>: agenda →
    formulation → adoption → implementation → evaluation. You have already met the executive —
    the Prime Minister, the Cabinet, the civil service — as a set of institutions. Here you see
    those institutions <em>in motion</em>: where their decisions come from, why some ideas become
    law and most do not, and where the gap between announcement and reality opens. The textbook
    draws the cycle as a neat circle; the reality is messier — windows open rarely,
    implementation rewrites intent, and evaluation rarely kills its targets. Studying the cycle
    is studying where things actually go wrong.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the five stages and their known failure modes" widget="equationStep" config={policyCfg} />

  <h2>Agenda-setting: the hardest stage</h2>
  <p>
    More policy decisions are made by <em>not</em> deciding than by deciding. At any moment a
    government could act on air quality, pension age, dental health or football governance —
    and acts on almost none. John Kingdon's multiple-streams model (1984) explains what
    selects the winners. Three streams run independently: a <strong>problem</strong> stream (indicators,
    crises, feedback), a <strong>solution</strong> stream (designs waiting in departments, think tanks
    and academia), and a <strong>politics</strong> stream (elections, public mood, media). When they
    align — a crisis makes a problem visible, a ready plan exists, the politics permits it — a
    <strong>policy window</strong> opens briefly and major change happens fast.
  </p>
  <p>
    Two British cases show the mechanism. In December 1952 the Great Smog killed thousands in
    London (later analyses put the toll around 12,000); coal smoke was the obvious cause, and
    the Clean Air Act of 1956 followed within four years — after a decade in which smoke
    abatement bills had repeatedly failed. And after the Dunblane school shooting in March
    1996 (sixteen children and a teacher killed), a handgun ban passed in 1997 — legislation
    that had been stuck for years. Notice what each case takes: not new information, but
    attention. The solution stream often predates the window — the civil service had smoke
    control drafts ready for years, which is why reading "sudden" reforms backwards usually
    finds the waiting solution.
  </p>
  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> media coverage does not simply <em>cause</em> policy. It raises
    problems onto the agenda, but a window still needs a workable solution and political
    willingness — scandals that had neither (countless inquiries "accepted in full" and then
    quietly shelved) show all three streams are required.
  </p>

  <h2>Formulation, adoption, and who decides</h2>
  <p>
    Once a problem is on the agenda, options get drafted — mostly by <strong>civil servants</strong>
    (impartial, permanent, advising under the Westminster convention), increasingly with think
    tanks, special advisers and outside lobbyists in the room. Adoption is where
    <strong>veto points</strong> matter. The UK concentrates power: a government with a Commons
    majority can normally pass its programme, because the unelected House of Lords can only
    delay (the Parliament Acts of 1911 and 1949) and there is no separate elected president to
    veto anything. The US, by contrast, has a president, two equal chambers and a Supreme
    Court willing to strike laws down — which is why the same problem produces bolder policy
    in London and stalemate in Washington. This concentration is a double-edged sword: it let
    the Thatcher government introduce the community charge ("poll tax", Scotland 1989,
    England and Wales 1990) quickly — and it meant the design flaws (regressive bills,
    uncollectable debts, the Trafalgar Square riot of March 1990) could not be stopped, the
    tax was scrapped by 1993, and the backlash helped end Thatcher's premiership.
  </p>

  <h2>Two theories of how decisions actually get made</h2>
  <p>
    Behind the cycle sits a fight about rationality. The <strong>rational-comprehensive
    model</strong> says decision-makers should define goals, list every option, cost each, and
    choose the optimum. Herbert Simon demolished that as psychology: humans have
    <strong>bounded rationality</strong> — limited time, information and attention — so they
    <em>satisfice</em>, taking the first option that is good enough (work that won him the 1978
    Nobel prize in economics). Charles Lindblom's reply (1959, "The Science of Muddling
    Through") turned the limitation into a theory: real policy advances by
    <strong>incrementalism</strong> — small adjustments to what exists, negotiated between
    partisans, because big radical designs outrun anyone's ability to predict their effects.
    The garbage-can model (Cohen, March and Olsen, 1972) is the anarchic limit case:
    solutions, problems and decision-makers float independently and sometimes collide by
    chance. The realistic stance: governments <em>should</em> be rational, are in fact
    incremental, and the policy windows of the last section are the moments when the
    incremental fog briefly lifts.
  </p>

  <h2>The executive: structure, role and powers</h2>
  <p>
    Who runs the cycle? The <strong>executive</strong>: the Prime Minister and Cabinet at the core,
    roughly a hundred ministers and junior ministers around them, and a civil service of
    several hundred thousand officials who draft, cost and implement. Its powers come in three
    flavours. <strong>Statutory powers</strong> are granted by Acts of Parliament. The
    <strong>royal prerogative</strong> — the residue of medieval monarchical power — still lets
    ministers declare war, sign treaties, appoint ministers and (in practice) manage the
    timing of elections, all without a vote; Parliament has clawed some back (the 2011
    Fixed-term Parliaments Act, itself repealed in 2022) and the courts have policed the rest:
    in <em>Miller</em> (2017) the Supreme Court ruled that triggering Brexit required Parliament's
    approval, and in <em>Miller II</em> (2019) it ruled the five-week prorogation of Parliament
    unlawful — a unanimous verdict that prerogative power is reviewable. Third,
    <strong>party power</strong>: because the PM leads the majority party, patronage over careers
    converts into votes in the Commons.
  </p>

  <h2>The Prime Minister and the Cabinet</h2>
  <p>
    Is Britain governed by the Cabinet (a committee of equals, as classical textbooks said) or
    by the Prime Minister? The honest answer: it depends on the PM's political resources.
    Cabinet ministers head big departments and can mobilise party support; the PM controls
    patronage (appointing and sacking them), the Cabinet Office agenda, the machine of
    committees, and above all the media narrative. Prime ministers who command their party —
    Thatcher early, Blair with big majorities — run a <strong>prime-ministerial</strong> style:
    Blair's "sofa government" decided in informal bilaterals, and the Iraq War decision (2003)
    was taken with a tiny circle, though a Cabinet (and Parliament) formally endorsed it.
    Prime ministers who lose their party — Thatcher by the end, through the exit-interview
    logic of cabinet resignation — discover the system's parliamentary core. The UK is
    therefore neither pure cabinet government nor a presidency: it is a parliament-centred
    system whose centre of gravity moves with one politician's standing.
  </p>

  <h2>Ministerial responsibility: the accountability mechanism</h2>
  <p>
    Two conventions tie the executive to Parliament. <strong>Individual responsibility</strong>:
    a minister answers to Parliament for the personal conduct and the failings of their
    department — and resigns when the failure is grave. The founding case is Crichel Down
    (1954), where minister Sir Thomas Dugdale resigned although officials, not he, had
    mismanaged the land deal — establishing that responsibility does not evaporate on the way
    down the building. The modern case is Amber Rudd's resignation in April 2018 over the
    Windrush deportations. But the convention is a convention, not a law: Priti Patel was
    found to have breached the Ministerial Code in 2020 and kept her job with the PM's
    backing — which is exactly why critics want the code's adviser independent of the PM.
    <strong>Collective responsibility</strong>: Cabinet decisions are binding on all ministers, who
    must defend them publicly or resign — Robin Cook's resignation over the Iraq War (2003) is
    the textbook case. It can be <em>suspended</em>: for the 2016 EU referendum, ministers were
    allowed to campaign on opposite sides, an exception that shows the rule exists to protect
    government unity, not truth.
  </p>

  <h2>Implementation and evaluation: where intentions dissolve</h2>
  <p>
    A decided policy still has to be delivered by real organisations with real constraints.
    Michael Lipsky (1980) called the people who do this — teachers, police officers, benefits
    clerks — <strong>street-level bureaucrats</strong>: they face more cases than resources allow, so
    they ration, simplify and improvise, and their routines become the policy citizens
    actually meet. The clearest recent British example is Universal Credit, the merger of six
    benefits into one monthly payment: designed to "make work pay", it was rolled out from
    2013 with endless delays, and the National Audit Office (2018) found that despite
    simplification the system risked <em>costing more</em> to administer than the old schemes and
    had pushed many claimants into hardship and food-bank use. Design intent did not survive
    contact with delivery.
  </p>
  <p>
    Evaluation is the weakest link. Good practice exists: randomised controlled trials have
    migrated from medicine into policy — the Education Endowment Foundation has funded
    hundreds of trials in English schools since 2011, and the Behavioural Insights Team
    randomises letters and reminders routinely. But the incentives run the other way: the
    department that launched a programme has an interest in its survival, "sunk cost" psychology
    protects failed pilots, and the political cycle rewards announcement over measurement.
    Honest evaluation therefore needs protection — independent offices (like the UK Statistics
    Authority or the Office for Budget Responsibility), sunset clauses, and pre-registered
    success criteria agreed <em>before</em> launch.
  </p>
  <p>
    The evaluation record also shows how intuition fails. The American "Scared Straight"
    programmes — taking young offenders into prisons to be frightened out of crime — felt
    obviously effective and were widely adopted; randomised trials assembled in a 2002
    Cochrane review found they made participants <em>more</em> likely to offend, not less. The
    school drug-prevention programme DARE was similarly popular for decades while
    meta-analyses found negligible effects on drug use. Both cases teach the lesson the
    clinical-trials lesson taught in medicine: plausible storytelling is not evidence, and the
    programmes that survive honest testing are often duller than the ones that die.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    The analytic layer underneath the cycle is comparative failure analysis. <strong>Market
    failure</strong> (monopoly, externalities like smoke, public goods, information problems)
    justifies intervention; but <strong>government failure</strong> — public-choice theory's
    contribution — catalogues the state's own failure modes: rent-seeking, information
    problems, capture by the regulated, short electoral horizons. The mature question is never
    "markets or government?" but "which institution fails less <em>for this problem</em>, and can
    we design around its failure mode?" Add <strong>policy transfer</strong>: ideas travel between
    countries — some because they work (smoke control, seat-belt laws), others because they
    are fashionable (zero-tolerance policing spread as much by narrative as by evidence), and
    some fit the borrowing context while others (institutional transplants) rot on arrival.
    One more modern layer: since the 1980s "New Public Management" tried to import private-sector
    disciplines — targets, performance pay, contracting-out — with mixed results, as the
    target-setting scandals (hospitals gaming waiting lists) showed that measuring one number
    reliably distorts the rest of the job.
    The open question the field is working on: can evaluation be built into policy
    <em>constitutionally</em> — so that killing your own failed programme is rewarded rather than
    punished? Until it can, expect the cycle to keep producing confident launches, quiet
    failures and lessons learned by whoever comes next.
  </p>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Kingdon, Agendas, Alternatives, and Public Policies (1984); Lipsky, Street-Level Bureaucracy (1980); Howlett &amp; Ramesh, Studying Public Policy; National Audit Office on Universal Credit (2018); R (Miller) v Secretary of State (2017, 2019).</p>
